                     <note>The substantive <foreign>peḍhikā</foreign> (then spelt <foreign>peḍhīkā</foreign>) is also found in composition in <ref target="DHARMA_INSKanaganahalli00289.xml">KnI0289</ref>, l. 1, and in <ref target="DHARMA_INSKanaganahalli00312.xml">KnI0312</ref>, l. 2. Cf. AMg <foreign>peḍhiyā</foreign> = Skt. <foreign xml:lang="san-Latn">pīṭhikā</foreign> (see also <foreign xml:lang="san-Latn">pīṭhī</foreign>), meaning “seat, base, pedestal”. See, for instance, NWS, s.v. <bibl><ptr target="bib:Sircar1966_01"/></bibl>, s.v. gives “platform”. Could this point architecturally to a base? On the stool being mentioned in the context of Awakening, see for instance DN-a I.57-58.</note>
                 </app>
